The NoJIT interpreter runs MMX and SSE shifts by a register, packs and conversions (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0024)

NFSU2 stopped in the simulator before its window: its CRT checks the
FPU and SSE exception masks with a block FEX's interpreter could not run
("[interp] guest block 0x75eb50 needs IR op VUShrSWide"). The
interpreter lacked the IR of 29 instructions a game of that time uses:

- VUShlSWide, VUShrSWide, VSShrSWide: psllw/psrlw/psraw and their d/q
  forms by an MMX or XMM register (the shift is its low 64 bits);
- VSQXTN, VSQXTN2, VSQXTNPair, VSQXTUN, VSQXTUN2, VSQXTUNPair: packsswb,
  packssdw, packuswb, packusdw, pmaddubsw;
- VUShrNI, VUShrNI2: pmulhrsw, and the cvt*2dq range checks;
- VSSHLL, VSSHLL2, VUABDL, VUABDL2: psadbw, mpsadbw;
- VSRSHR, VSQSHL: psignb/w/d;
- VFAddP, VFAddV: haddps/haddpd, dpps/dppd;
- VFRecpPrecision, VFRSqrtPrecision, VFCopySign: 3DNow! reciprocals;
- VFCVTL2, VFCVTN2, Vector_FToI, Vector_FToISized, Vector_F64ToI32,
  VSToFVectorInsert, VFToIScalarInsert: roundps/pd/ss/sd, cvtps2dq,
  cvtpd2dq, cvtpd2pi, cvtpi2ps, cvtss2si and the like.

Each follows the arm64 JIT's code for a host without SVE, as the rest of
the interpreter does: narrowing ops take the 128-bit source and write the
low 64 bits, Vector_F64ToI32 rounds, narrows to a float and truncates as
fcvtn + fcvtzs do, frint32x/frint32z give the most negative integer for
NaN and out of range values, and the reciprocals are exact (no
FEAT_RPRES on Apple M1/M2).

